<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uncle Tom's Cabin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uncle Tom's Cabin is an American anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> novel by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harriet-beecher-stowe">Harriet Beecher Stowe</a>, serialised in The National Era from 1851 and published in two volumes in 1852. Stowe wrote it in response to the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850, and it sold 300,000 copies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in its first year. The best-selling novel of the 19th century in the United States, it is credited with helping fuel the abolitionist cause in the 1850s. The book and the plays it inspired also helped popularise negative stereotypes of black people, including the &quot;Uncle Tom&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uncle-toms-cabin.jpg" alt="Uncle Tom's Cabin" width="320" height="546" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harriet Beecher Stowe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harriet_Beecher_Stowe,_Uncle_Tom's_Cabin,_vol._1,_1852,_title_page_(clean).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harriet-beecher-stowe">Harriet Beecher Stowe</a>, a Connecticut-born teacher at the Hartford Female Seminary, belonged to the religious Beecher family and was an active abolitionist. She wrote a sentimental novel to depict the horrors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> while asserting that Christian love could overcome it. It first appeared as a 40-week serial in The National Era, an abolitionist periodical, from 5 June 1851 to 1 April 1852. It was meant to run for only a few weeks, but Stowe expanded it, and protests reached the office when she missed an issue. The character of Eliza was inspired by an account of a slave woman who crossed the frozen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a> in February 1838.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book opens in Kentucky, where the farmer Arthur Shelby, facing the loss of his farm through debt, sells two of his slaves to the slave trader Mr. Haley: Uncle Tom, a middle-aged man with a wife and children, and Harry, the young son of Emily Shelby's maid Eliza. Eliza overhears the plan and flees with her son across the ice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a>. Tom is taken down the river to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, and after his owner St. Clare dies, he is sold to the plantation owner Simon Legree.</p><p>Legree beats Tom when he refuses to whip a fellow slave, and tries to crush his faith, but Tom keeps reading his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and comforting the others. Eliza's family reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Liberia, and George Shelby frees the slaves of the Kentucky farm. Stowe's central theme is the evil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, above all the separation of families, together with the power of Christian love.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>It was the best-selling novel and second best-selling book of the 19th century in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, and it is credited with helping fuel the abolitionist cause. Defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> protested, and seven Anti-Tom novels appeared in 1852 and six more in 1853. A story that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> greeted Stowe in 1862 with the words 'So this is the little lady who started this great war' is likely apocryphal. Far more Americans saw the story on stage than read it, in 'Tom shows' such as George L. Aiken's version, the most popular play in the United States and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for 75 years.</p><p>The novel is a landmark of protest literature, and later books such as The Jungle by Upton Sinclair and Silent Spring by Rachel Carson owe it a large debt. It has also been criticised for racist descriptions of its black characters and for stereotypes it helped fix, and the Tom of the title came to mean an excessively subservient person. James Baldwin, in 1945, called it a bad novel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uncle Tom's Cabin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Fugitive Slave Act of 1850, Harriet Beecher Stowe’s <b>Uncle Tom’s Cabin</b> of 1852 and John Brown’s raid on the federal armoury at Harpers Ferry in 1859 deepened the crisis.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Harriet Beecher Stowe's <b>Uncle Tom's Cabin</b> (1852) has Tom sing it, with a verse not by Newton that had been passed down orally in African American communities.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li></ul></section>
